<%#
  A box you paste a secret into. One definition, rendered by all three panels,
  because what is written here is a rule about how this console handles secrets
  and three copies of it in three gems is three chances to drift.

  Locals: id, name. Optional: describedby, disabled, value, hint.

  ---

  It LOOKS like a password field and is not one, and that is the whole point.

  `type="password"` is what makes a browser offer to save the value to the
  user's password manager, and offer to fill it back in on the next visit.
  Neither is wanted here. An API key is not a credential for THIS site -- it is
  a value belonging to Postmark or Stripe that happens to be typed into a form
  -- so "save password for 127.0.0.1?" is an offer to file it under the wrong
  name, in a vault it will later be autofilled from into the wrong box.

  `autocomplete="off"` does not fix it and never did. Every major browser
  deliberately ignores it on password fields, because sites used it to stop
  people using a password manager at all; the override is the browser siding
  with its user, and it is right to. Nothing you can put on a
  `type="password"` field turns the prompt off.

  So the field is `type="text"` and the masking comes from CSS instead. Same
  round dots, and to the browser's heuristics it is an ordinary text box.

  Where that leaves us if the CSS is unsupported: the value is legible on
  screen. `-webkit-text-security` is a non-standard property, though every
  current engine takes it -- Chrome, Safari and Edge for years, Firefox since
  119. The degradation is a secret visible to somebody looking at a screen that
  is already showing a development console served only to this machine, which
  is a fair trade for never handing the value to a password manager.

  The `data-*` attributes are each one vendor's documented opt-out, for the
  managers that scan fields themselves rather than trusting the browser.
  Harmless where unrecognised.

  ---

  `hint` is the last four characters of what is already stored, and it goes in
  the PLACEHOLDER.

  It answers the only question this box raises when something is already in it:
  which key is in there. The tick beside the label answers whether one is.

  Placeholder rather than value, and that is what makes the whole thing safe.
  A value would be submitted like any other text, so a developer who opened the
  page to read it and pressed Save would overwrite a live key with whatever
  decoration we had put in the box -- a save that succeeds, says so, and breaks
  sending. A placeholder is never submitted. The box posts blank, and
  Settings.write already drops a blank secret rather than erasing a stored one,
  which is a rule that was there before this and needs nothing added to it.

  It also disappears on focus, which reads as a flaw and is the correct
  behaviour: the moment you are typing is the moment the old key stops being
  what the box is about.

  Legible despite the masking, because `-webkit-text-security` applies to the
  value and not to the ::placeholder pseudo-element -- checked in the browser
  rather than assumed, since a masked hint would have been four more dots and
  no information.
%>
<input type="text"
       id="<%= id %>"
       name="<%= name %>"
       value="<%= local_assigns[:value] %>"
       <%= tag.attributes(placeholder: "••••#{local_assigns[:hint]}") if local_assigns[:hint].present? %>
       autocomplete="off"
       spellcheck="false"
       autocapitalize="off"
       autocorrect="off"
       data-1p-ignore
       data-lpignore="true"
       data-bwignore
       data-form-type="other"
       <%= tag.attributes(aria: {describedby: local_assigns[:describedby]}) if local_assigns[:describedby].present? %>
       <%= "disabled" if local_assigns[:disabled] %>
       <%#
         The placeholder is not decoration here -- it carries which key is
         stored -- so it meets the same 4.5:1 as body text rather than the
         washed-out grey a placeholder usually gets. slate-400 was written here
         first and measures 3.01:1 on the dark field.
       %>
       class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 [-webkit-text-security:disc] placeholder:text-slate-600 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:placeholder:text-slate-300 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
